An Activity Diagram is a behavioral diagram within UML (Unified Modeling Language) that models the procedural flow of control
While a Sequence Diagram focuses on how components talk to each other over time, an Activity Diagram focuses on the operational steps, choices, and parallel execution paths of a particular use case.

Core Components :
Initial State (Filled Circle): 
Marks the absolute starting point of the workflow.
Action / Activity (Rounded Rectangle): 
Represents a single, discrete step or execution block in the process. simple bsic activity like password authentication
Control Flow (Solid Arrow): 
Shows the sequential path from one action to the next.
Decision Node (Diamond): 
A branching point with guard conditions written in brackets [like this]. Only one path can be taken at a point of time
Merge Node (Diamond): Brings multiple alternative decision paths back into a single sequence. once the decisions evaluated and then if redirected to same flow
Fork (Thick Horizontal or Vertical Black Bar): 
Splits a single execution flow into two or more parallel paths that run concurrently. kind of go routines
Join (Thick Horizontal or Vertical Black Bar): 
Synchronizes multiple parallel paths back together. The flow cannot move past the Join bar until all incoming parallel activities finish.
Final State (Bullseye / Filled Circle inside an outline): Marks the termination of the entire workflow.